file C:\Users\dalbrecht\Research
    Projects\ManyDaughters\ManyDaughters_DataPreprocessing/data/derived_confi
    > dential/5%-sample/h4_dataset.dta saved

. 
. 
. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. 
. * FILTERS * 
.  
.         * Example: only keep participants from private households
.         keep if inlist(pop,1,2)
(4,698 observations deleted)

.         keep if sexor==0 // keeping hetero individuals
(5,192 observations deleted)

.         keep if biovalid==31 // keeping parents (dropping people without chil
> dren)
(10,795 observations deleted)

.         keep if nett1==1 // keeping subjects with successful interview
(562 observations deleted)

.         keep if syear==2018 // we test this hypothesis in the last available 
> data for which the outcome is available: 2018
(13,143 observations deleted)

.         bysort hid (syear): gen byte hid_to_drop = (sumkids[_N] > 3)

.         bysort hid (syear): replace hid_to_drop = 1 if (sumkids[_N-1] > 3) //
>  dropping few      cases in which the two parents give different reports
(308 real changes made)

.         drop if hid_to_drop ==1 // drop families that on the last wave report
>  4 children or more
(408 observations deleted)

. 
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES *
. 
.         gen age = 2025-gebjahr

.         gen age2 = age^2

.         gen number_daughters=0

. 
.         foreach i of numlist 1/19 {                // loop over 01 … 19
  2.                 local v = "kidsex" + string(`i',"%02.0f")
  3.                 quietly replace number_daughters = number_daughters + (`v'
>  == 2)
  4.         }

.         
.         gen d_daughter = (number_daughters>=1 & number_daughters!=.) // any d
> aughter? conditional on having any children

. 
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H4 * 
. 
.         * The outcome is the extent to which participants agree with the stat
> ement that it is "Best if man and woman work the same amount so they can shar
> e the responsibility" (plh0308_v2). This outcome being a higher number, on a 
> likert scale from 1 to 7, indicates greater support for gender equality.
. 
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear OLS regression analysis with plh0308_v
> 2 as dependent variable and the dummy variable d_daughter interacted with gen
> der of the parent as independent variables. We control for  number of childre
> n fixed effects, sex, whether the participant was born in Germany, age and ag
> e^2.
. 
. 
.         gl add_controls germborn age age2

.         reg plh0308_v2 d_daughter##ib2.sex i.sumkids $add_controls if plh0308
> _v2>=0, vce(robust)

Linear regression                               Number of obs     =        379
                                                F(8, 370)         =       2.42
                                                Prob > F          =     0.0146
                                                R-squared         =     0.0389
                                                Root MSE          =     1.7414

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
             |               Robust
  plh0308_v2 | Coefficient  std. err.      t    P>|t|     [95% conf. interval]
-------------+----------------------------------------------------------------
